    "brief": "I have all four research agents' findings. This is a synthesis task — no further investigation needed. Let me write the brief.\n\n# SEED-001: On-Demand Power-Cycling the Production Phala TEE CVM — Decision Brief\n\n## 1. Workload shape\n\nThe TEE worker does exactly one production job: signing IPNS records on the republish cron. It is otherwise idle — a persistent Express server with no internal scheduler, woken only when the API's BullMQ cron POSTs `/republish`.\n\n- **Cadence:** fixed cron `0 */6 * * *` → 4 runs/day (00:00, 06:00, 12:00, 18:00 UTC).\n- **Per-run duration:** up to 2000 due entries → up to 20 sequential TEE batches of 100. Healthy-case execution is sub-second per batch (signing is ms; the 30s timeout is a degraded-path ceiling). Realistic healthy run: **well under a minute**; pathological fully-degraded run: up to ~10 min.\n- **Freshness deadline:** records are signed with a **48h EOL**, republished every 6h → **8x safety margin**. The hard requirement is that each record be re-signed within 48h or resolvers treat it as expired.\n\n**Duty cycle:** ~4 runs/day × a few active minutes ≈ **4–10 active minutes/day out of 1440 ≈ 0.3–0.7%**. The CVM is paying always-on rates to do <1% work. The economic motivation is real.\n\n## 2. The core feasibility question: key continuity (make-or-break)\n\nThis is the crux, and the news is **favorable but needs one runtime confirmation**.\n\nWhat we know with high confidence from the code + dstack KMS docs:\n\n- The worker holds **no persisted private key**. Keys are **deterministically derived per epoch** via `DstackClient.getKey('cipherbox/ipns-republish', epoch-${N})`, bound to the CVM **app_id**.\n- dstack KMS keys are derived from a stable identity (`SealingKey = KDF(RootKey, deployer_id, app_hash, nonce, …, epoch)`). The same keys **re-derive after stop/start** of the same CVM, and per Phala docs even after destroy/recreate **of the same app identity**, because the root key is replicated to backup KMS nodes.\n- **The known landmine:** destroy-and-recreate that yields a *new* app_id changes `teePublicKey` for every epoch → **all previously ECIES-wrapped IPNS keys become permanently undecryptable**. This is documented inline as a CRITICAL rule in `docker-compose.phala.yml` and in `35-RESEARCH.md`.\n\n**Implication for on-demand:** `stop/start` sidesteps the landmine entirely — it preserves app_id, instance-id (seed persisted), and therefore all epoch keys. This is the safe primitive. The teePublicKey the API persisted in `tee_key_state` stays valid; the soft epoch-mismatch warning in `initializeFromTee()` should not fire.\n\n**What must be verified (not yet proven in-repo):**\n- The *raw attestation quote* is NOT byte-stable across boots (RTMR3/mr-kms changes per boot) — but this **does not matter for us** because **there is no attestation/quote verification anywhere in the codebase** (repo-wide grep for `getQuote|attestation|dcap|RA-TLS` → zero hits). Clients trust `teePublicKey` as served by the API. So quote instability is a non-issue *today*. (It would become a blocker only if attestation is ever added.)\n- Confirm empirically that `phala cvms start` after a stop re-derives the identical `getKey()` output (i.e. the same `teePublicKey` per epoch). High confidence from docs, but unverified against our specific app.\n\n## 3. Cost model\n\n- **Phala bills per-second, no monthly minimum**, for regular CPU/TDX CVMs (small $0.06/hr, medium $0.12/hr, large $0.23/hr).\n- **STOPPED CVM:** compute charges stop immediately; **storage still bills** (~$0.10/GB/mo) to preserve the encrypted disk. Only **DELETE** halts storage too.\n- **GPU TEE has a 24h minimum re-applied each launch** → cycling is only economical for **CPU/TDX** CVMs. Our worker is CPU/TDX, so we're in the favorable class.\n\n**Rough savings (assume medium, $0.12/hr ≈ $86/mo always-on):**\n- At ~0.5% duty cycle, compute drops to a few cents/mo — but **cold-start time dominates the bill, not execution** (see §4). If each wake costs ~5 min running × 4/day = 20 min/day ≈ $0.04/day ≈ **~$1.20/mo compute** + storage. If cold-start is ~20 min (large image), 4 wakes/day ≈ 80 min/day ≈ **~$5/mo** + storage.\n- **Net: ~85–95% compute reduction**, bounded below by storage (~$0.10/GB/mo) and by cold-start duration.\n\n**Honest unknowns:** exact CVM size/disk size for prod isn't pinned in repo (image tag may default to `latest`), and we don't know the disk footprint, so the storage floor is a guess. The savings are real but the absolute dollar figure is unconfirmed — and on a single staging-sized CVM the absolute saving may be **single-digit dollars/month**, which is the honest framing of the prize.\n\n## 4. Cold-start vs deadline\n\nThis is where on-demand is **comfortably safe on the deadline but the cost win hinges on image size**:\n\n- **Cold-start = image pull + decompression.** Small image: ~1–2 min. A 1.4GB image: **~20–25 min** (decompression-bound). Execution itself is ms.\n- **Deadline math:** the freshness window is 48h with a 6h cadence. Even a 25-min cold-start is **trivially within budget** — you'd start the CVM, wait for `/health` to report ready, then POST `/republish`. There is ~5.5h of slack before the *next* cron, and ~47h before any record actually expires.\n\nSo **latency is not a risk to freshness**. Cold-start matters only because (a) it lengthens the billable active window (eroding savings if the image is large) and (b) it adds orchestration complexity (the API must wait-for-ready before dispatching). **Action item:** measure our actual image size and real `phala cvms start` time, and confirm whether `start` re-pulls or uses a cached layer.\n\n## 5. Architecture options\n\n**A. Stop/start the same CVM (recommended primitive).**\nOrchestrator: before a republish run, `phala cvms start app_<id>`, poll `/health` until ready, run batches, then `phala cvms stop`. Preserves app_id → keys safe. Tradeoff: still pays storage; needs the API (or a thin scheduler) to own start/stop + readiness gating, and to handle the cron→start coupling. **Lowest key-continuity risk, modest savings.**\n\n**B. Destroy/recreate with same app identity.**\nRemoves storage cost too. Tradeoff: **high risk** — must guarantee app_id/identity is preserved across recreate (docs say KMS re-authorizes same-identity, but this is the exact path that bricked-keys warning is about). Not worth it for ~$0.10/GB/mo of storage savings. **Reject unless storage cost is shown to be material.**\n\n**C. Keep always-on but downsize.**\nIf prod is medium/large, drop to small ($0.06/hr ≈ $43/mo). Zero new orchestration, zero key risk, captures a chunk of the savings. Tradeoff: leaves money on the table vs stop/start but is **near-zero-effort and zero-risk**. Strong \"do this first\" baseline.\n\n**D. Serverless / scale-to-zero TEE.**\nPhala has **no first-party scale-to-zero or cron CVM feature** — any on-demand cycling is custom orchestration we build and maintain. Tradeoff: most savings, most engineering + most operational surface (a failed `start` now means a missed republish). **Not justified at current scale.**\n\n## 6. Open questions / spikes before committing\n\nSmallest experiments, in order:\n\n1. **Key-continuity spike (blocking):** On a throwaway TDX CVM, derive `getKey()` for an epoch, `phala cvms stop` → `start`, re-derive, assert identical `teePublicKey`. ~30 min. This is the one true make-or-break check.\n2. **Cold-start measurement:** Time `phala cvms start` → `/health` ready for our actual image; note whether `start` re-pulls. Tells us both the savings ceiling and the orchestration timeout. ~30 min.\n3. **Disk/storage footprint + CVM size:** Pull `phala cvms get` for prod to learn size, disk GB, and image tag → turns the cost model from estimate into a number. ~10 min.\n4. **Prod-vs-staging CVM identity:** Resolve the open question of whether `cipherbox-tee-staging` (CIPHERBOX_ENVIRONMENT=staging) **is** production or a distinct prod CVM exists. Don't cycle the wrong one. ~10 min.\n5. **Orchestration ownership + failure mode:** Decide who calls start/stop (API service vs external scheduler) and what happens if `start` fails — the DB backoff state machine already handles a failed batch (retrying → stale after 10 fails), so a missed wake degrades gracefully within the 48h window, but this should be explicit before shipping.\n\n## 7. Recommendation\n\n**Key continuity is almost certainly fine for stop/start** (deterministic same-identity KMS keys, no attestation verification to break, no persisted secret), and **cold-start is a non-issue against a 48h deadline**. The concept is sound.\n\n**But be honest about the prize:** on one staging-sized CVM the absolute saving is likely **single-digit dollars/month**, and on-demand cycling adds a new failure surface (the republisher can now be *asleep* when it's needed) plus orchestration the API doesn't have today.\n\n**Highest-value next step:** do **Option C now** (downsize the always-on CVM if it isn't already `small`) — it captures most of the dollar savings at zero risk and zero new infra. In parallel, run **spike #1 (key continuity) + #2 (cold-start)**; if both pass cleanly, graduate to **Option A (stop/start)** behind the existing DB-backoff safety net. **Do not pursue B or D** at current scale — the savings don't justify either the key-bricking risk (B) or the custom serverless surface (D).\n\nRelevant files: `apps/tee-worker/src/services/tee-keys.ts`, `apps/tee-worker/docker-compose.phala.yml`, `apps/api/src/tee/tee.service.ts`, `apps/api/src/tee/tee-key-state.service.ts`, `apps/api/src/republish/republish.service.ts`, `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md:145-149`.",
